  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1054 AM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nTODAY:\n\nA low pressure system continues to drop down the West Coast today. \nThis will transition the parent flow to southwesterly, which will \nadvect upper level moisture into the region. This will increase \ncloud cover over the coming days, but keeps the moisture confined to \nthose upper levels. Therefore, a pleasant fall day is on tap today.\n\nISOLATED TO SCATTERED SHOWERS FRIDAY AND SATURDAY:\n\nA wave rotating around the parent low will provide enough lift to \nkick off showers beginning Friday afternoon and lasting through \nSaturday. PWAT values are projected to be upwards of 200% of normal, \nalthough dry surface levels will take time to fully saturate. \nPrecipitation totals through Saturday will be on the lighter side as \na result, with predominantly rain showers, with some snow flurries \npossible above 10,000 ft. There is a possibility for a few stray \nthunderstorms, which would be capable of producing localized heavier \nrainfall across the region. \n\nWETTER AND UNSETTLED BY SUNDAY AND MONDAY:\n\nA stronger moisture push associated with an atmospheric river (AR) \narrives by Sunday. This AR will begin taking a southern track \nthrough Baja California, propagating through the Desert Southwest. \nThere continues to be disagreements between both the ECMWF and GFS \nmodels. The ECMWF insists on bringing the moisture plume further \nnorth, while the GFS confines the highest PWATs to the Four Corners \nregion. Therefore, there are still some uncertainties when it comes \nto the extent of this moisture. Either way, this is an impressive \namount of moisture that, when combined with enhanced lift ahead of \nan approaching low, will result in widespread precipitation. Models \ndo still agree on an area of 1-2 inches of rainfall across southwest \nColorado and southeast Utah Sunday into Monday. Chances for river \nflooding remains low at this point, as these totals will be spread \nover an almost 48 hour period. The bigger concern lies with flash \nflooding across areas with already saturated soils from last week's \nrainfall, particularly for areas of steep terrain, and over recent \nburn scars. See the Hydrology section below for additional \ninformation. \n\nAdditionally, a cold front pushes through from northwest to \nsoutheast Sunday night into Monday, with snow levels dropping to \naround 8,000 ft by Monday morning, and close to 7,000 ft by the \nevening as colder air pushes behind the front. Snow could be heavy \nat times. Mountain passes will likely see impacts, with some travel \nslowdowns for motorists Monday morning and into the afternoon. \nPrecipitation will taper off Monday night, with colder and drier \nconditions anticipated Tuesday and beyond. Daytime highs on Monday \nand Tuesday will run close to 10 degrees below normal, with \ntemperatures remaining in this range through much of next week.",
